To achieve this, you can use Google Apps Script to fetch data from a sheet and perform a geocoding search using the Google Maps API. Here's a sample script:

```javascript
function getLatLong() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSheet();
  var dataRange = sheet.getRange("A1:C10"); // Assuming your data is in columns A to C, rows 1 to 10
  var data = dataRange.getValues();

  for (var i = 1; i < data.length; i++) {
    var placeName = data[i][0]; // Assuming the first column contains place names
    var url = "https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?address=" + placeName + "&key=YOUR_API_KEY";

    var response = UrlFetchApp.fetch(url);
    var json = JSON.parse(response);

    if (json.results.length > 0) {
      var latitude = json.results[0].geometry.location.lat;
      var longitude = json.results[0].geometry.location.lng;

      data[i][1] = latitude; // Assuming the second column will contain latitudes
      data[i][2] = longitude; // Assuming the third column will contain longitudes
    } else {
      data[i][1] = "";
      data[i][2] = "";
    }
  }

  sheet.getRange("D1:D" + (data.length + 1)).setValues(data); // Assuming the fourth column will contain latitudes
}
```

Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with your actual Google Maps API key. This script will loop through the specified range, fetch the latitude and longitude of each place name using the Google Maps API, and store the results in the corresponding columns.

