Yes, you can achieve this using SQL database by creating a trigger on the "work_sessions" table. A trigger is a special kind of stored procedure that automatically executes when a specific event occurs, such as inserting or updating data. In this case, we will use a trigger to check if 10 minutes have passed since the start_time of the current work session and prompt you to take a break.

Here's an example of how you can create a trigger for this purpose:

```sql
CREATE TRIGGER take_break AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE ON work_sessions
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN
    DECLARE start_time TIMESTAMP;
    DECLARE end_time TIMESTAMP;

    SELECT start_time, end_time INTO start_time, end_time FROM work_sessions WHERE id = NEW.id;

    IF (TIMEDIFF(MINUTE, start_time, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP) >= 10) THEN
        -- Prompt user to take a break
        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('It has been 10 minutes since you started working. Take a break!');
    END IF;
END;
```

This trigger will execute after each insert or update operation on the "work_sessions" table. It will retrieve the start_time and end_time of the current work session and check if 10 minutes have passed since the start_time. If so, it will prompt you to take a break.

Note that this trigger assumes that you are using a database management system that supports triggers, such as Oracle Database or MySQL. The exact syntax may vary depending on the database management system you are using.

Also, keep in mind that this trigger will only run if you are actively working on the database. If you are not using the database, the trigger will not execute. To ensure that the trigger runs continuously, you can use a scheduler or a cron job to execute the trigger at regular intervals.

