Here's an example of how you can display the set of prime numbers and calculate their product using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Prime Numbers</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
        }

        h1 {
            text-align: center;
        }

        ul {
            list-style: none;
            padding: 0;
        }

        li {
            margin-bottom: 5px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Prime Numbers</h1>
    <ul>
        <li>3</li>
        <li>7</li>
        <li>13</li>
        <li>19</li>
    </ul>
    <script>
        function calculateProduct() {
            const primeNumbers = [3, 7, 13, 19];
            let product = 1;

            for (let i = 0; i < primeNumbers.length; i++) {
                product *= primeNumbers[i];
            }

            return product;
        }

        const productElement = document.createElement('p');
        productElement.textContent = `The product of the prime numbers is ${calculateProduct()}.`;
        document.body.appendChild(productElement);
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code creates an unordered list (`<ul>`) containing the prime numbers (3, 7, 13, and 19). It then uses JavaScript to calculate the product of these numbers and displays the result as a paragraph element (`<p>`) at the bottom of the page.

