To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can implement a simple collision detection system. When generating a new planet, check if it's within a certain distance from any existing planet. If it is, generate a new planet until one is found that doesn't collide with any other planet.

Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function with collision detection:
```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
      const distance = planet.position.map((p, j) => Math.abs(p - planets[i].position[j]));
      if (distance.reduce((a, b) => a + b) < minDistance) {
        continue;
      }
    }

    planets.push(planet);
  }

  return planets;
};
```
This function will keep generating new planets until it finds ones that don't collide with each other. You can adjust the `minDistance` variable to control the maximum distance between planets.

Here's a simple React utility to remove widows in your text. It will split the text into sentences and check if any sentence is wider than the container width. If it is, it will wrap the sentence in a `div` element with a class name of `widow`. You can then style the `widow` class to adjust the layout as needed.

```jsx
import React from 'react';

const WidowRemover = ({ text, containerWidth }) => {
  const sentences = text.split('.');
  const widowedSentences = [];

  sentences.forEach((sentence) => {
    const words = sentence.split(' ');
    const sentenceWidth = words.reduce((acc, word) => acc + word.length, 0);

    if (sentenceWidth > containerWidth) {
      widowedSentences.push(sentence);
    }
  });

  return (
    <div>
      {sentences.map((sentence, index) => (
        <React.Fragment key={index}>
          {widowedSentences.includes(sentence) ? (
            <div className="widow">{sentence}</div>
          ) : (
            sentence
          )}
          <br />
        </React.Fragment>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};

export default WidowRemover;
```

